# A CONTAINER CONSTRUCTION'S ARGUMENTS ARE ITS `create`'S ARGUMENTS. Every
# container family declares `create: function {capacity: 0.u64}`, and both ways
# of writing a construction are that call: the arguments after the parenthesised
# type, `(ListVal t: i64) capacity: 8`, and a `capacity:` written inside the type
# application, `(ListVal t: i64 capacity: 8)` or bare `ListVal u32 capacity: 8`.
# Neither was checked against the declaration: a string or a name nothing
# declares reached the C, and a label `create` does not have was dropped
# without a word.
main: function is {
    a: (ListVal t: i64) capacity: "big"
    b: (ListRef T: String) capacity: ghost
    c: (MapVV k: i64 v: i64) cap: 8
    d: (SetVal k: i64) capacity: "big"
    e: (MapRV K: String v: i64) size: 4
    f: (ListVal t: i64 capacity: "big")
    g: (SetRef K: String capacity: ghost)
    h: ListVal u32 capacity: "big"
    i: IdSet u64 capacity: phantom
    j: IdMapV u64 v: i64 capacity: "big"
    print "\{a.length} \{b.length} \{c.length} \{d.length} \{e.length}"
    print "\{f.length} \{g.length} \{h.length} \{i.length} \{j.length}"
}
